# Line-ending policy for adaptive-math-platform.
#
# Why this file exists: several artefacts in this repository record a SHA-256 over
# raw file bytes -- the runtime-bundle fixtures (tests/fixtures/runtime_bundle/*.json
# against test_catalogue.md), the OpenAPI contract freeze, and the live-engine wheel's
# SOURCE_MANIFEST.txt, which hashes each shipped .py "as shipped". Those checksums were
# computed over LF bytes. With core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout rewrites text files
# to CRLF, so the same content hashes differently and the wheel's own sha256 becomes
# dependent on which machine built it -- destroying the property the hash pin exists to
# guarantee. Normalising to LF everywhere, in the index and in the working tree, removes
# that entire class of failure.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary artefacts must never be touched. The pinned live-engine wheels are hash-verified
# in requirements.lock, the provenance manifest, the Dockerfile, .dockerignore and three
# runtime-bundle fixtures; any conversion would corrupt them irrecoverably.
*.whl binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# The vendored governed catalogue is hash-pinned to bytes that are NOT LF-normalised, so the
# blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above must not reach it.
#
# `c-runtime-bundle-approved-1.0.json` records `catalogue.checksum` =
# 82004a5fef94528ea1e4187473420453bdfb7e1d3b5ade48ce2bc03486630851, computed over the governed
# source in ResearchProject/Yass/Component_C_v1.0/, which is CRLF. Normalising this copy to LF
# changes every line by one byte -- 2133 lines, 2133 bytes, a different sha256 -- and
# `bundle_resolution.py` then raises `RuntimeBundleChecksumMismatchError`, which
# `CSessionService.create` converts into a bare HTTP 409 with no indication of the cause. Every
# Component C session fails, and the error names nothing.
#
# That is not hypothetical: it is exactly what happened once the normalisation rule above first
# reached a working tree, taking 33 backend tests with it. `-text` pins the bytes so this copy
# stays byte-identical to the artefact its checksum attests to.
#
# If this file is ever re-vendored, copy it verbatim and re-verify the sha256 against the bundle
# BEFORE committing -- do not "fix" a mismatch by editing the recorded checksum, which would
# assert that governance signed bytes it never saw.
backend/provenance/R26-IT-138_Component_C_Game_Task_Catalogue_v1.0.md -text
